patterns.explanation:
Many of this week's rules cover only part of the activity they target, which creates predictable leakage channels:
- the 10 MW exemption in Korea, an invitation to bunch just below it;
- 50% coverage of international voyages in the UK ETS, an incentive to restructure port calls;
- the EU plastic waste ban, which still allows exports to OECD countries and could reroute trade through them;
- uneven national enforcement of the EUDR, a route for importers through weakly policed member states;
- Washington recycled-content fines small enough to treat as a cost of business;
- the 20% capex threshold in SFDR 2.0, an invitation to cluster just above it.

Each comes with a dated effective date, an advantage for difference-in-differences or bunching designs. The shared threat is that the diversion itself is hard to observe.
